a81c47bf (3221 1792934933287 "guenchi" (("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . doc) (class . ruling) (title . "Invariant: the cut-keyed caches never answer for a working view") (keywords . "defs-index header-table children-table overlay working view cache identity") (text . "The definitions index (store.sc defs-index), the file-header table (header-table) and the children table (children-table) are kept for the committed store by the applied token, then the applied cut, and the language catalogue's generation. A working view is an overlay of drafts at the committed cut (reduce.sc reduction-overlay?) and a new state on every call, so a cut-keyed entry would answer it with the committed store's definitions or with an earlier working view's. Therefore an overlay never reads or fills those caches. It has slots of its own: one for the definitions index (overlay-index-state) and one for the two tables (overlay-tables-slot), each holding the last overlay asked about, compared by eq? identity and by the catalogue's generation, and replaced when another overlay asks. The two slots are independent of each other, so up to two working views stay resident per process. No working ask changes what the committed caches hold. The children table's own slot is not pinned by any row: the rows below draft definitions and headers, never a change to which children a file has.\n\nBlocks: 4gm4c2jr.17b (store.sc: overlay-state?, defs-index, committed-defs-index); w5vbequy.23j (store.sc: overlay-tables, header-table, committed-header-table, children-table).\n\nPinned by test/context-verb.sc, block w5vbequy.42z: CW-9 (the committed context asked first, then --working, finds the drafted definition g; --working asked first, then the committed context, has no g and still f); CW-10 (two writers at the same cut each see their own definition and not the other's); CW-11 (a drafted header import: the working context brings lc's f and not lb's, and the committed context is unchanged); CW-12 (the same writer's second draft at the same cut, asked right after the first: --working finds j and not g, and the committed context has neither and still f).\n\nWhat reads red when it breaks: an overlay sent through the cut-keyed definitions index (CW-9 both rows, CW-10); the header-table bypass removed while the children table keeps its own (CW-11); the overlay mark never set, so every overlay is treated as committed (CW-9, CW-10, CW-11); the overlay index slot compared by applied cut instead of identity (CW-10, CW-12).\n\nEstablished by the landing of 2026-10-25, theourgia master fbb79c6 (search over the dispatcher's state; context --working).") (parent . root) (ord . 947/2))))
4c0b4c6e (3222 1792934933393 "guenchi" (("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . doc) (class . ruling) (title . "Invariant: a view that is not the publication never touches the kept query session") (keywords . "make-session-provider query session kept cell fresh cut working view daemon") (text . "The daemon's query-session provider (query.sc make-session-provider, installed by daemon.sc at the first published read) keeps one session for the published state. Its rule: a view that is not eq? to the published state gets a fresh session over that view and the kept cell is neither read nor written; the decision is (fresh cut), or (fresh disabled) when the provider's bound is 0, which hands every view a fresh session before the identity is asked. A working view is a new reduction on every call (working.sc working-state overlays the drafts on a fresh open-and-reduce), so it is never eq? to the publication and always takes this rule; context --working relies on it and adds no branch of its own. Only a view eq? to the publication reaches the kept cell. There the derived generation, the catalogue's generation, the size bound and a moved cut may drop it, and then the session is reused (the same view), advanced to the new publication (advanced), or replaced by a fresh session that is kept (fresh none, fresh not-forward, fresh advance-refused, fresh nothing-held).\n\nBlocks: 4gm4c2jr.1bg (query.sc: make-session-provider, the second clause of provide).\n\nPinned by test/daemon-session.sc: SESS-W, block 4gm4c2jr.1cv (committed context, then two context --working calls, then committed again through the daemon: the working calls are (fresh cut), and the committed call after them reuses the kept session with nothing expanded); SESS-5, block 4gm4c2jr.1cz (in process: a view that is not the publication gets a fresh session over that view and leaves the kept one, which the next call over the publication reuses).\n\nWhat reads red when it breaks: the rule disabled, so that any view reaches the kept cell (SESS-W, SESS-5). context-verb's CW rows do not pin it: they call rpc-dispatch in process, where no provider is installed.\n\nEstablished by the landing of 2026-10-25, theourgia master fbb79c6, which kept this existing rule as the working view's route.") (parent . root) (ord . 1421/3))))
eeeb6e5d (3223 1792934933394 "guenchi" (("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . doc) (class . ruling) (title . "Invariant: search answers over the dispatcher's state") (keywords . "search search-state-report store-search-report publication dispatcher limit") (text . "The search verb (rpc.sc, the search entry of the verb table) answers over the state the dispatcher handed it, taken through reduction-for as every read verb takes it, by calling store.sc search-state-report. On a resident daemon that state is the publication, so a search neither opens nor reduces the store. search-state-report applies the same limit rule as store-search-report (check-search-limit: #f for every hit or a positive exact integer, anything else refused naming the report asked) and runs the same search over the state it is given. So at one state, for the same query, the same limit and the same hooks (the keyword hook, and the validity hook that --all-validity sets), the answer's bytes do not depend on which report was asked; an invalid limit is refused by either, naming the report asked. Between the daemon and the local route the equality is what A-7 pins: the same request, populated, empty, capped and uncapped, answers the same text.\n\nBlocks: w5vbequy.1zx (rpc.sc: the verb table, its search entry); w5vbequy.23r (store.sc: store-search-report, search-state-report, check-search-limit).\n\nPinned by test/read-receipt.sc C48a-1, block w5vbequy.8x4 (every verb that carries a cut, search among them, answers (cut ...) equal to the applied cut); and by test/daemon-session.sc A-6, A-7, A-8, block 4gm4c2jr.197 (two searches through the socket in a row are both routed to the connection and neither opens the log; the socket's --wire text of a search equals the local route's text, populated and capped at ten hits with (truncated (hits 4)); while a reload is held, search answers the publication that read answers).\n\nWhat reads red when it breaks: search sent back to store-search-report, so that it opens and reduces the store on every search (measured red on A-6, A-8 and C48a-1 in the round that established it). The limit rule itself is pinned by test/cli3.sc N9, which has no edge here.\n\nEstablished by the landing of 2026-10-25, theourgia master fbb79c6.") (parent . root) (ord . 1895/4))))
